<section>
  <h2>Fit: code scales to the available space</h2>

  <p>
    Code is scaled to fit the slide (between half and 1.6x). The <b>largest</b> frame of a section decides,
    so the first frame below looks small: the next one is 16 lines long. Press → and it stays the same size.
  </p>
  <code lang=typescript id=g1>
    <frame>
      const a = 1;
    </frame>
    <frame>
      const a = 1;
      const b = 2;
      const c = a + b;
      const d = c * 2;
      const e = d - a;
      const f = e / b;
      const g = f % 3;
      const h = g ** 2;
      const i = h + 1;
      const j = i + 2;
      const k = j + 3;
      const l = k + 4;
      const m = l + 5;
      const n = m + 6;
      const o = n + 7;
      const p = o + 8;
    </frame>
  </code>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><c>size=</c> fixes the font size</h2>

  <p>Two frames, <c>size=30</c>: the code is big and stays big.</p>
  <code lang=typescript id=g2 size=30>
    <frame>
      const big = "size=30";
    </frame>
    <frame>
      const big = "size=30";
      const bigger = big + "!";
    </frame>
  </code>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><c>fit=none</c></h2>

  <p>No automatic scaling at all (scale 1). Use it with a fixed <c>size</c> when you control the content.</p>
  <code lang=typescript fit=none>
    const plain = "scale 1, no fit";
  </code>
</section>

<notes>
  The largest frame of a section decides the scale, so the font size is constant across frames.
</notes>
